---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import { DOCS_URL, GITHUB_URL } from '../../consts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Overview — DeepCausality"
  description="Learn how DeepCausality connects observations, causal rules, and outcomes in Rust. Explore service root-cause analysis, then explore context, uncertainty, and the wider ecosystem."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
      <h1>From observations to decisions</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        DeepCausality is an open-source Rust library for modeling cause and effect.
        You write the rules, connect them into a model, and evaluate what follows from your inputs.
        The model can change as new data arrives or the system’s conditions evolve.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        Start with one rule. Add context, uncertainty, or actions when your problem needs them.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>An alert tells you something is wrong. What caused it?</h2>
      <p>
        A service slows down, and several metrics change at once. An anomaly detector can
        flag the incident, but an engineer still needs to know where to investigate.
        The root-cause analysis example connects these two tasks: detect an anomaly,
        then rank the service metrics that could explain it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It uses recorded telemetry from the Sock Shop microservice benchmark: 44 metrics
        covering CPU, memory, workload, and latency. A small machine-learning model built
        with Candle scores the telemetry. DeepCausality uses that score to decide whether
        to run causal analysis, carrying the decision, configuration, and log through one process.
      </p>

      <figure class="reasoning-flow reticle-host" aria-labelledby="flow-caption">
        <span class="reticle reticle-tl" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="reticle reticle-tr" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="reticle reticle-bl" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="reticle reticle-br" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="eyebrow">Detect → decide → investigate</p>
        <ol class="flow-steps">
          <li><span class="step-label">1. Detect</span><strong>Score the service telemetry</strong><span>The Candle model produces an anomaly score for a window of measurements.</span></li>
          <li><span class="step-label">2. Decide</span><strong>Does the score warrant investigation?</strong><span>Below the threshold, return a healthy verdict. At or above it, run causal analysis.</span></li>
          <li><span class="step-label">3. Explain</span><strong>Rank candidate root causes</strong><span>DeepCausality’s BRCD algorithm analyzes normal and anomalous data using a supplied causal graph.</span></li>
          <li><span class="step-label">4. Report</span><strong>Return the leading candidate and decision log</strong><span>The example checks the ranked metric against the dataset’s expected result.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption id="flow-caption">
          The causal-analysis branch runs only when the detector’s score reaches the threshold.
          See the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/examples/causal_discovery_examples/ml/ml_rca`}>complete
          ML and causal root-cause analysis example</a>, including data, source, and run instructions.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The framework’s role is to connect detection to investigation: a
        <code>PropagatingProcess</code> carries the score, window identity, configuration,
        and decision log. Its next step either skips the analysis or returns a root-cause
        verdict; an analysis failure travels through the same process as an error.
        You can replace the detector while retaining this pattern.
      </p>
      <p>
        This example demonstrates the integration on recorded data. Its threshold is calibrated
        from the supplied normal and anomalous windows to exercise both paths; it is not a
        measurement of production detection accuracy.
      </p>

      <h2>Three terms you’ll meet</h2>
      <p>The service example composes detection and analysis through a process. For models that
        also need identifiable reasoning units or a shared environment, the library provides
        Causaloids and Context.</p>
      <dl class="concepts">
        <div>
          <dt><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/causaloid/`}>Causaloid: a unit of reasoning</a></dt>
          <dd>A function wrapped with an identity and description. It can check a threshold,
            run a calculation, or participate in a larger model. Causaloids can be combined
            into collections and graphs.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/context/`}>Context: the information a rule depends on</a></dt>
          <dd>An explicit structure for data, time, space, and relationships that rules can consult.
            Use it when several reasoning steps need a shared view of changing conditions.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/effect-propagation-process/`}>Effect: what a step passes on</a></dt>
          <dd>The carrier for a result, together with information such as errors and logs.
            Passing effects between functions lets you assemble a computation from smaller steps.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h2>What makes a model dynamic?</h2>
      <p>
        A fixed model always uses the same rules and relationships. A dynamic model can update
        its context or causal structure while the system runs. For example, a monitor can use
        different limits for different operating conditions, or add a new sensor to its model.
      </p>
      <p>
        DeepCausality provides the structures for these changes; you define when and how they
        happen. You can also compare alternative inputs to ask a counterfactual question:
        “What would this model predict if one condition were different?”
        The <a href="/examples/pearl-counterfactual/">counterfactual example</a> shows this in code.
      </p>

      <h2>Choose the tools your problem needs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Build a causal model.</strong> Use <code>deep_causality</code> to connect
          reasoning steps, maintain context, and evaluate outcomes.</li>
        <li><strong>Explore relationships in data.</strong> The
          <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/cdl/`}>Causal Discovery Language</a> provides a pipeline
          for loading data, selecting features, and analyzing candidate causal relationships.</li>
        <li><strong>Represent uncertain inputs.</strong> The
          <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/uncertainty/`}>uncertainty library</a> lets you work with
          distributions and values that may be absent.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect results to actions.</strong> Use a
          <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/csm/`}>Causal State Machine</a> to pair reasoning with
          actions. Add <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/effect-ethos/`}>Effect Ethos</a> to check
          proposed actions against rules you encode. Those checks enforce the supplied policy;
          they do not establish that the policy covers every real-world risk.</li>
        <li><strong>Build scientific computations.</strong> The project also provides Rust crates
          for linear algebra, tensors, geometry, calculus, and physics. Use them independently
          or combine them with causal reasoning. Explore the
          <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/uniform-math/`}>mathematical foundation</a> and
          <a href="/projects/">CFD and quantum projects</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A model’s conclusions depend on its rules, assumptions, and data. Running a causal
        model does not by itself establish that those relationships hold in the real world.
      </p>

      <h2>The theory behind the library</h2>
      <p>
        DeepCausality implements the <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/effect-propagation-process/`}>Effect
        Propagation Process</a> (EPP): each step derives a new effect from a previous effect
        through a causal function. The mathematical formulation describes how these steps compose.
        You can begin with the examples and return to the
        <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/concepts/axiom/`}>axiom</a> and
        <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/overview/architecture/`}>architecture</a> when you want the details.
      </p>

      <h2>Start with one working example</h2>
      <ol class="next-steps">
        <li><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/getting-started/install/`}>Install the library</a> in a Rust project.</li>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/getting-started/hello-causaloid/`}>Write your first Causaloid</a> to see how an input becomes a result.</li>
        <li><a href="/examples/">Browse the worked examples</a> for aerospace, counterfactuals, and scientific computing.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        DeepCausality is an LF AI &amp; Data Foundation Sandbox project.
        Explore the <a href={GITHUB_URL}>source on GitHub</a> or
        <a href="/community/">ask the community</a> about your use case.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .reasoning-flow {
      margin: var(--space-6) 0;
      padding: var(--space-5);
      background: var(--bg-1);
      border: 1px solid var(--line-1);
      border-radius: var(--radius-md);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-1);
    }
    .reasoning-flow .eyebrow { color: var(--fg-1); }
    .flow-steps { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); padding: 0; margin: var(--space-5) 0; list-style: none; }
    .flow-steps li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding-top: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
    .step-label { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); color: var(--fg-1); }
    .flow-steps strong { color: var(--fg-0); }
    figcaption { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
    .concepts { margin: var(--space-5) 0; }
    .concepts > div { border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); padding: var(--space-4) 0; }
    .concepts dt { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
    .concepts dd { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-1); }
    .next-steps { padding-left: var(--space-5); }
    @media (min-width: 720px) {
      .flow-steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
